1
00:00:00,070 --> 00:00:04,520
Welcome to Video 3. You’ve built
your story and made it believable.

2
00:00:04,520 --> 00:00:08,960
Now, let’s make sure people
stay to hear it with the hook.

3
00:00:08,960 --> 00:00:11,960
You have about 10 seconds. Watch the clock.

4
00:00:11,960 --> 00:00:16,210
That’s roughly how long an audience gives
you before they decide whether to keep

5
00:00:16,210 --> 00:00:19,640
listening. Your opening has to earn the rest.

6
00:00:19,640 --> 00:00:21,320
Here’s how many demos start.

7
00:00:21,320 --> 00:00:26,320
“Hi, I’m Alex, and today I’ll show you
my app, which has three features.”

8
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:29,890
It’s polite, but it’s all
about you and your app.

9
00:00:29,890 --> 00:00:34,180
Nobody has a reason to care yet,
and you can feel the room drift.

10
00:00:34,180 --> 00:00:38,760
Now, try this. “Amara arrived
at midnight to an empty flat.

11
00:00:38,760 --> 00:00:41,800
No pot. No lamp. No one to ask.”

12
00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:44,960
Straight away, there’s a person and a problem.

13
00:00:44,960 --> 00:00:49,680
The audience leans in, because they
want to know what happens next.

14
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:54,990
The data backs this up. Viewers leave
fastest in the very first seconds.

15
00:00:54,990 --> 00:01:00,700
Short videos of 1–2 minutes lose far
fewer people at the start than long ones.

16
00:01:00,700 --> 00:01:06,040
So get to the point, show rather than
tell, and don’t start with yourself.

17
00:01:06,040 --> 00:01:08,320
There are four kinds of hook you can use.

18
00:01:08,320 --> 00:01:11,640
Pick one. Each takes 10–15 seconds.

19
00:01:11,640 --> 00:01:17,040
The first is a moment. Drop the viewer
straight into your persona’s scene.

20
00:01:17,040 --> 00:01:20,160
“It’s 2 a.m. and Amara’s plane just landed.”

21
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:24,350
It’s the easiest hook, because
it leads right into your story.

22
00:01:24,350 --> 00:01:28,920
The second is a question. One the
viewer answers in their head.

23
00:01:28,920 --> 00:01:31,880
“What was the first thing
you needed in a new home?”

24
00:01:31,880 --> 00:01:35,320
The moment they answer, they’re
thinking about your problem.

25
00:01:35,320 --> 00:01:38,040
The third is a surprising fact.

26
00:01:38,040 --> 00:01:40,920
One number that makes people sit up.

27
00:01:40,920 --> 00:01:46,160
Use a real figure from a named study
and put its source and year on screen.

28
00:01:46,160 --> 00:01:51,270
The fourth is a what-if. Paint the
world your product makes possible.

29
00:01:51,270 --> 00:01:55,440
“What if every new student could borrow
what they need in their first week?”

30
00:01:55,440 --> 00:01:58,360
Whichever you choose, follow these rules.

31
00:01:58,360 --> 00:02:01,080
Start with a person or a problem, not the app.

32
00:02:01,080 --> 00:02:06,800
Give your name and your product’s name after
the hook, and keep it under 15 seconds.

33
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:10,320
Think of it this way. Your hook is a promise.

34
00:02:10,320 --> 00:02:12,680
The rest of your demo keeps it.

35
00:02:12,680 --> 00:02:17,680
Now it’s your turn. One, write
three hooks for your project.

36
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:22,840
Each a different kind. Two, read
each one aloud to a partner.

37
00:02:22,840 --> 00:02:28,640
Three, an hour later, ask which one
they remember, and keep that one.

38
00:02:28,640 --> 00:02:33,600
Worksheet 4 in the handbook
has room for all three.
